
Strings

    What:
        A string is a sequence (array) of characters terminated by a null character '\0'.
        In C, strings are stored as character arrays.

    Why:
        To represent and manipulate textual data – names, messages, files, etc.
        Strings are essential for user interaction, data processing, and communication.

    Where:
        Everywhere – reading input, printing output, storing text, file handling, etc.

    How:
        Declare a character array large enough to hold characters + '\0'.
        Use standard library functions or custom loops to process them.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Declaration and Initialization

        Static Array:
            char str[100];                     // 100 characters (uninitialised)
            char name[] = "Hello";             // size = 6 (5 chars + '\0')
            char name[10] = "Hello";           // remaining elements are '\0'

        Dynamic Allocation:
            char *str = (char*)malloc(100 * sizeof(char));

        Strings are always null‑terminated. The null character marks the end of the string.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Memory Representation

        Example: char str[] = "ABC";

            Index:   0   1   2   3
            Value:  'A' 'B' 'C' '\0'

        The array name 'str' represents the base address of the first element.
        Hence, in scanf() we write  scanf("%s", str)  without &.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Input / Output Methods

        • printf("%s", str)      – prints the string until '\0'
        • scanf("%s", str)       – reads a word (stops at whitespace)
        • gets(str) / fgets()    – reads a whole line (gets is unsafe, fgets preferred)

        • %c  – reads a single character (including whitespace)
          Example: scanf(" %c", &ch);   // leading space skips previous whitespace

        • Scansets – read only specified characters or stop at certain characters.
          Syntax: %[characters]  or %[^characters]

          %[a]       -> reads only 'a'
          %[!a]      -> same as %[^a]? (implementation defined; avoid)
          %[^a]      -> reads all characters EXCEPT 'a'; stops when 'a' appears

          %[A-Z]     -> reads uppercase letters only
          %[a-zA-Z]  -> reads alphabets only
          %[0-9]     -> reads digits only
          %[^\n]     -> reads until newline (reads the entire line including spaces)

          The scanset works like a set: [^...] negates the set.
          When the next character does NOT belong to the set, scanf stops reading.

          How [^r] works internally (bitwise XOR example):
            Input "Hello World", scanset %[^r]  -> reads until 'r' is encountered.
            For each character c, if (c ^ 'r') == 0  (i.e., c == 'r'), stop.
            This is how the delimiter decides to stop.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Why Not Use & with String Name in scanf("%s", str) ?

        str is an array name and evaluates to the address of its first element.
        Passing str is equivalent to passing &str[0].
        If we write &str, we get the address of the entire array, which is the same address
        but with a different pointer type (pointer to array vs pointer to char).
        Using &str in scanf("%s") is incorrect and may cause warnings/undefined behaviour.

        Example:
            char str[100];
            scanf("%s", &str[5]);   // reads a word and stores it starting at index 5
            printf("%s", &str[5]);  // prints from index 5 onwards

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    String Pointers

        A pointer to a character can be used to handle strings:

            char *ptr = "Hello";        // ptr points to a read‑only literal
            char str[] = "Hello";       // str is a modifiable array copy

        With a pointer, you can traverse the string:
            while (*ptr) {
                putchar(*ptr);
                ptr++;
            }

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Useful Format Specifier: %n

        %n stores the number of characters successfully read so far into an integer pointer.
        Example: scanf("%s%n", str, &count);  // count now holds the length of the string read

        This can be used to find string length without strlen() during input.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Common Standard Library Functions (<string.h>)

        strlen(str)           – returns length of string (excluding '\0')
        strcpy(dest, src)     – copies src to dest
        strcat(dest, src)     – concatenates src at end of dest
        strcmp(s1, s2)        – compares two strings (0 if equal)
        strchr(str, ch)       – returns pointer to first occurrence of ch
        strstr(haystack, needle) – finds substring

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Custom Operations (Typical Problems)

        • Reverse the whole string
        • Reverse each word in a string (word by word)
        • Reverse ignoring special characters (special reverse)
        • Length of string (using loop, %n, or pointer)
        • Sorting characters in a string (e.g., "engineering" → "eeeggiinnnr")
        • Searching for a character or substring
        • Replacing characters (e.g., vowels with '#')
        • Case conversion (upper to lower, lower to upper, toggle)
        • Deleting specific characters or duplicates
        • Frequency count of each character
        • Split a camel‑case string into words ("thisIsAnAutomationEra" → "this is an automation era")
        • Base conversion (e.g., "23GF" in hex to decimal)

